fix(pip): Drive night vision for the lens as well, and record the optic facts in the log

Auditing the imaging family after the thermal change - the review rule being that a fix has to
be followed by a pass over everything related - showed the same gap on the night vision side:

  * ps_r2_nightvision, like ps_r2_heatvision, is never set by anything in the engine for an
    equipped optic, while in the PiP branch the lens shader no longer produces the look itself
    (the stock path calls infrared() on the G-buffer and the second-view branch skips it).
  * The pack provides the fact through its device bridge script (scope_lense_head_nvg_active /
    scope_lense_head_thermal_active) and lets the player refuse it (scope_lense_allow_nvg /
    scope_lense_allow_thermal), so the renderer now honours all of them and restores the
    player's own setting when the lens goes away.

scope_lense_imaging is derived by its binder from those two console variables, so publishing
them is what makes the shader skip its own grade for an image that already carries the effect.
An earlier attempt in this change added a device field for the same information; the audit
showed it would have been redundant state next to the variable the binder already reads, so it
was dropped rather than left to drift.

The per optic diagnostic now records the weapon section, the optic item section, the resolved
magnification, the lens FOV, the dynamic zoom flag, and the thermal flags with their mode, so a
log answers which optic was used instead of requiring the player to remember.

Audit of the constant set this round (every scope_lense_* the lens material reads): values and
sources match the contract, m_hud_fov_params.z is never written which is what selects the
direct projection branch, and scope_lense_quality.yz correctly reports the sampled texture
dimensions - the same extent the shader's texel math needs, an independent confirmation that
reverting the capped lens extent was necessary.